meta {
  name: Push (drain)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/v1/stourify/sync/push
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{token}}
}

headers {
  X-Organization-Id: {{org_uuid}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "sto_spots": {
      "created": [
        {
          "uuid": "3f8c1b40-6a2e-4d55-9c1a-000000000001",
          "title": "Offline-made spot",
          "description": "Created on the plane; drained on landing.",
          "latitude": 6.1164,
          "longitude": 125.1716,
          "address": "Kalaklan, General Santos City",
          "categories": ["coast", "nature"],
          "status": "published"
        }
      ],
      "updated": [],
      "deleted": []
    }
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.data.server_time: isDefined
}

docs {
  The offline-sync drain. Mirrors the delta shape: a bucket per pushable table
  (everything except sto_cities, which is reference data).

  Every row carries a CLIENT-generated `uuid`, and that uuid is the identity —
  the server resolves by it, so replaying this exact request creates nothing new.
  Send it twice to see the idempotency guarantee: the second run returns
  op "created", status "ok", and the same record.

  Each operation is independent. A rejected row comes back as
  { status: "rejected", reason: "validation" | "forbidden" | "error", errors }
  in `data.results` while its siblings still apply — a bad row never 4xx's the
  batch. Every write runs the same FormRequest rules and the same policies a
  single POST /api/v1/spots would, through CrudService; push is not a bypass.
  `user_id` is always forced to the caller, whatever the row claims.

  The response envelope IS `{ data: … }` here (unlike the delta), because the
  client reads the push response with `get`, not `getRaw`.
}
